  1115→\paragraph{Bottleneck.}
  1116→Table~\ref{tab:comparison_bottleneck} and Figure~\ref{fig:bottleneck_comparison} compare the methods on the bottleneck system ($A \to B \to B + C$, $k_1 = 10^{-6}$, $k_2 = 0.1$) over $[0, 10^4]$. This system provides the clearest demonstration of connectivity preservation. The flux-based adaptive FSP uses fixed steps $\delta t = 10$ (1{,}000 iterations), completes in 0.4\,s, and achieves $\langle C \rangle = 4.97$ at the final time. The Krylov-FSP-SSA method completes in $<0.1$\,s (100 iterations, $\tau = 100$) but produces $\langle C \rangle \approx 0$, completely missing~$C$ production.
  1117→
  1118→The discrepancy arises because after a single step of $\tau = 100$\,s, the bottleneck state $(0, 1, 0)$ carries probability $p(B{=}1) \approx k_1 \tau = 10^{-4}$, well below the Krylov-FSP-SSA pruning threshold $\varepsilon/|\mathcal{S}| \approx 10^{-2}/4 \approx 2.5 \times 10^{-3}$. Without flux protection, this state is pruned every iteration, severing the only pathway through which probability can flow from the $A = 1$ initial condition to the $C$-producing reaction $B \to B + C$. The flux-based method retains $(0, 1, c)$ because its outgoing flux $p(B{=}1) \times k_2$ is large relative to the total system flux, despite the low probability.
